  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "algebraic : false$\nLz : matrix([0,-1,0],[1,0,0],[0,0,1-1])$      /* L₃ from 5.01 */\n\n/* --- exp is not injective --- */\nprint(\"NOT INJECTIVE.  exp(2πn L₃) for n = 0..3:\")$\nfor n : 0 thru 3 do\n  print(\"   n = \", n, \" : \", tidy( matrixexp(2*%pi*n*Lz) ))$\nprint(\"   An entire lattice in so(3) is crushed onto the identity.  exp is\")$\nprint(\"   a diffeomorphism only NEAR 0; the kernel is what the Lie algebra\")$\nprint(\"   cannot see, and it is exactly the global information of 5.01.\")$\n\n/* --- exp is not surjective on SL₂(ℝ) --- */\n/* For traceless X the eigenvalues are ±λ with λ² = a² + bc, so\n   tr exp(X) = e^λ + e^{−λ} = 2 cosh λ.  If λ is imaginary this is 2 cos|λ|.\n   Either way tr exp(X) ≥ −2. */\nXg : matrix([aa,bb],[cc,-aa])$\nTg : ratsimp( mat_trace( matrixexp(Xg) ) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"NOT SURJECTIVE on SL₂(ℝ).  For traceless X, with λ = √(a²+bc):\")$\nprint(\"   tr exp(X) = \", Tg)$\nprint(\"   tr exp(X) − 2cosh λ = \",\n      ratsimp( exponentialize( Tg - 2*cosh(sqrt(aa^2+bb*cc)) ) ))$\nprint(\"   λ real      ⟹ tr = 2 cosh λ ≥ 2\")$\nprint(\"   λ imaginary ⟹ tr = 2 cos|λ| ∈ [−2, 2]\")$\nprint(\"   λ = 0       ⟹ tr = 2\")$\nprint(\"   so tr exp(X) ≥ −2 for every traceless X, with no exceptions.\")$\n\n/* A numerical sweep, wide enough to reach the imaginary-λ regime where the\n   bound is actually approached (λ near iπ). */\nlo : 1e9$  arg_lo : false$\nfor ia : -4 thru 4 do for ib : -4 thru 4 do for ic : -4 thru 4 do\n  block([M, T],\n    M : matrix([ia, ib],[ic, -ia]),\n    T : float( realpart( mat_trace( matrixexp(M) ) ) ),\n    if T < lo then ( lo : T, arg_lo : M ) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   sweeping a, b, c over integers −4 … 4 (729 matrices):\")$\nprint(\"      minimum of tr exp(X) = \", lo, \"   at X = \", arg_lo)$\nprint(\"      ≥ −2 ✓\")$\n\n/* the bound is attained exactly */\nXext : matrix([0, %pi],[-%pi, 0])$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   the bound is sharp: X = \", Xext)$\nprint(\"      exp(X) = \", tidy(matrixexp(Xext)),\n      \"   tr = \", ratsimp(mat_trace(tidy(matrixexp(Xext)))))$\n\nAnot : matrix([-2,0],[0,-1/2])$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   but A = \", Anot, \" has det = \", determinant(Anot),\n      \" and tr = \", mat_trace(Anot))$\nprint(\"   so A ∈ SL₂(ℝ) with tr A = −5/2 < −2: A is NOT exp(X) for any X.\")$\nprint(\"   SL₂(ℝ) is connected, yet its exponential map misses points.\")$\nprint(\"   (Compare SO(3): compact and connected, so exp IS onto — every\")$\nprint(\"    rotation has an axis and an angle, which is Euler's theorem, 1.03.)\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 4,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "NOT INJECTIVE.  exp(2πn L₃) for n = 0..3:\n   n =  0  :  matrix([1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,1])\n   n =  1  :  matrix([1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,1])\n   n =  2  :  matrix([1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,1])\n   n =  3  :  matrix([1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,1])\n   An entire lattice in so(3) is crushed onto the identity.  exp is\n   a diffeomorphism only NEAR 0; the kernel is what the Lie algebra\n   cannot see, and it is exactly the global information of 5.01.\nProviso: assuming 4*(bb*cc+aa^2) # 0\nNOT SURJECTIVE on SL₂(ℝ).  For traceless X, with λ = √(a²+bc):\n   tr exp(X) =  %e^-sqrt(bb*cc+aa^2)*(%e^(2*sqrt(bb*cc+aa^2))+1)\n   tr exp(X) − 2cosh λ =  0\n   λ real      ⟹ tr = 2 cosh λ ≥ 2\n   λ imaginary ⟹ tr = 2 cos|λ| ∈ [−2, 2]\n   λ = 0       ⟹ tr = 2\n   so tr exp(X) ≥ −2 for every traceless X, with no exceptions.\n   sweeping a, b, c over integers −4 … 4 (729 matrices):\n      minimum of tr exp(X) =  -1.9799849932008908    at X =\n                             matrix([0,-3],[3,0])\n      ≥ −2 ✓\n   the bound is sharp: X =  matrix([0,%pi],[-%pi,0])\n      exp(X) =  matrix([-1,0],[0,-1])    tr =  -2\n   but A =  matrix([-2,0],[0,-(1/2)])  has det =  1  and tr =  -(5/2)\n   so A ∈ SL₂(ℝ) with tr A = −5/2 < −2: A is NOT exp(X) for any X.\n   SL₂(ℝ) is connected, yet its exponential map misses points.\n   (Compare SO(3): compact and connected, so exp IS onto — every\n    rotation has an axis and an angle, which is Euler's theorem, 1.03.)"
     ]
