     "text": [
      "Proviso: assuming 4*th # 0\nAd_g(X) = g X g⁻¹ for g = exp(θL₃):\n   Ad_g(L₁) =  matrix([0,0,sin(th)],[0,0,-cos(th)],[-sin(th),cos(th),0])\n   still skew ?  true\n   Conjugation preserves the algebra, so Ad : G → GL(g) is a\n   representation on the 3-dimensional space so(3).\nad_X(Y) = d/dt|₀ Ad_{exp(tX)}(Y), with X = L₃ :\n   Y = L 1  :  derivative − [L₃, L 1 ] =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])\n   Y = L 2  :  derivative − [L₃, L 2 ] =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])\n   Y = L 3  :  derivative − [L₃, L 3 ] =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])\n   The bracket IS the derivative of conjugation.  That is what it\n   measures: the first-order failure of exp(tX) to commute with Y.\nmatrices of ad_{Lᵢ} in the basis (L₁,L₂,L₃):\n   ad_L 1  =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,-1],[0,1,0])    equals L 1  ?  true\n   ad_L 2  =  matrix([0,0,1],[0,0,0],[-1,0,0])    equals L 2  ?  true\n   ad_L 3  =  matrix([0,-1,0],[1,0,0],[0,0,0])    equals L 3  ?  true\n   so(3) acts on itself exactly as it acts on ℝ³: the adjoint\n   representation IS the standard one.  Consistent with 5.01 —\n   the hat map identifies so(3) with ℝ³ and the bracket with ×.\nAd_{exp X} = e^{ad_X}, both as 3×3 matrices in the basis:\nProviso: assuming 4*th # 0\n   Ad_{exp(θL₃)} =  matrix([cos(th),-sin(th),0],[sin(th),cos(th),0],[0,0,1])\n   e^{θ ad_{L₃}}  =  matrix([cos(th),-sin(th),0],[sin(th),cos(th),0],[0,0,1])\n   difference     =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])\n   An exponential in the GROUP equals an exponential in the ALGEBRA.\n   For so(3) both sides are the rotation matrix itself, because the\n   adjoint representation is the standard one."
