     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Z(t) = log( exp(tX) exp(tY) ), expanded to t³, versus\n   t(X+Y) + t²/2 [X,Y] + t³/12 ([X,[X,Y]] − [Y,[X,Y]])\nso(3), X = L₁, Y = L₂:\n   Z(t)        =\n                 matrix([0,-(t^2/2),t-t^3/12],[t^2/2,0,t^3/12-t],\n                        [t^3/12-t,t-t^3/12,0])\n   BCH to t³   =\n                 matrix([0,-(t^2/2),t-t^3/12],[t^2/2,0,t^3/12-t],\n                        [t^3/12-t,t-t^3/12,0])\n   difference  =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])\nsl(2,ℝ), X = e, Y = f:\n   Z(t)        =  matrix([t^2/2,t-t^3/6],[t-t^3/6,-(t^2/2)])\n   difference from BCH =  matrix([0,0],[0,0])\ncommuting case, X = diag(1,0), Y = diag(0,1):\n   [X,Y] =  matrix([0,0],[0,0])\n   exp(X)exp(Y) − exp(X+Y) =  matrix([0,0],[0,0])\n   With no bracket there is no correction, and the group looks\n   like a vector space.  Every term beyond X+Y in BCH is built\n   from brackets, so non-commutativity is the ONLY obstruction.\nConsequence: the group law near the identity is determined by the\nbracket alone.  Two groups with isomorphic Lie algebras are locally\nisomorphic — which is exactly why SU(2) and SO(3) differ only\nglobally, and why the 4π period of 5.01 is invisible to BCH."
     ]
    }
   ]
