   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## Summary\n\n$$\\mathrm{ad}_X(Y) = [X,Y] = \\frac{d}{dt}\\Big|_0 \\mathrm{Ad}_{\\exp(tX)}(Y), \\qquad \\mathrm{Ad}_{\\exp X} = e^{\\,\\mathrm{ad}_X}$$\n\n**Rodrigues, derived rather than quoted.** For a unit axis, $\\hat n^3 = -\\hat n$ — verified symbolically once the constraint $|n|^2 = 1$ was handed to the rational simplifier via `tellrat`. That cubic relation truncates the exponential series onto $I$, $\\hat n$, $\\hat n^2$, giving\n\n$$\\exp(\\theta\\hat n) = I + \\sin\\theta\\,\\hat n + (1-\\cos\\theta)\\,\\hat n^2.$$\n\nThe partial sum to $\\theta^8$ minus Rodrigues is identically zero, and `matrixexp` on a concrete axis agrees. It is the same mechanism as $e^{i\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + i\\sin\\theta$: a low-degree relation collapsing an infinite series.\n\n**Linear conditions from quadratic ones.** $\\det(\\exp X) = \\exp(\\operatorname{tr}X)$ was checked on a generic $2\\times2$ matrix with difference zero — which is *why* $\\operatorname{tr}X = 0$ is the infinitesimal form of $\\det A = 1$. The Rodrigues form was checked orthogonal with determinant 1 symbolically.\n\n**Where $\\exp$ breaks.**\n\n| failure | witness |\n|---|---|\n| not injective | $\\exp(2\\pi n L_3) = I$ for $n = 0,1,2,3$ — a whole lattice crushed to a point |\n| not surjective | $\\operatorname{diag}(-2,-\\tfrac12) \\in SL_2(\\mathbb R)$ is not $\\exp X$ for any $X$ |\n\nThe second has a clean proof: for traceless $X$ the eigenvalues are $\\pm\\lambda$, so $\\operatorname{tr}\\exp X = 2\\cosh\\lambda$ — verified symbolically — which is $2\\cos|\\lambda| \\ge -2$ when $\\lambda$ is imaginary and $\\ge 2$ when real. A sweep of 729 integer matrices found a minimum of $-1.98$, and $X = \\begin{pmatrix}0&\\pi\\\\-\\pi&0\\end{pmatrix}$ attains $-2$ exactly. Anything below $-2$ is unreachable. $SL_2(\\mathbb R)$ is connected and still $\\exp$ misses points; $SO(3)$ is *compact* and connected, so $\\exp$ is onto — which is Euler's theorem of 1.03.\n\n**The bracket is the derivative of conjugation.** $\\frac{d}{dt}|_0\\mathrm{Ad}_{\\exp(tL_3)}(L_j) - [L_3,L_j] = 0$ for all three $j$. And the matrices of $\\mathrm{ad}_{L_i}$ in the basis $(L_1,L_2,L_3)$ are **$L_i$ themselves**: the adjoint representation of $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$ *is* the standard representation, which is the same fact as 5.01's hat map. Then $\\mathrm{Ad}_{\\exp(\\theta L_3)}$ and $e^{\\theta\\,\\mathrm{ad}_{L_3}}$ came out equal — both the rotation matrix — with difference zero. An exponential in the group equalling one in the algebra.\n\n**BCH, verified to third order.**\n\n$$\\log(e^{tX}e^{tY}) = t(X+Y) + \\tfrac{t^2}{2}[X,Y] + \\tfrac{t^3}{12}\\big([X,[X,Y]] - [Y,[X,Y]]\\big) + O(t^4)$$\n\nmatched the truncated series exactly for $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$ ($X = L_1$, $Y = L_2$) and for $\\mathfrak{sl}(2,\\mathbb R)$ ($X = e$, $Y = f$), difference zero in both. With $[X,Y] = 0$ the correction vanishes entirely and $e^Xe^Y = e^{X+Y}$ on the nose.\n\n**So the group law near the identity is the bracket and nothing else.** That is the precise sense in which 5.01 lost no local information — and, since BCH is blind to the $4\\pi$ period of $SU(2)$, exactly how much it lost globally.\n\n## Next\n\n**5.03 — Invariant Metrics and Homogeneous Spaces** puts a metric on the group. Left-invariant metrics make $G$ a Riemannian manifold in its own right; bi-invariant ones (which exist iff $G$ is compact, up to abelian factors) make the group exponential and the Riemannian $\\exp_p$ of 4.03 coincide, and give a curvature formula $K(X,Y) = \\tfrac14|[X,Y]|^2$ — curvature straight out of the bracket. Then $G/H$ realises spheres, projective spaces and Grassmannians as quotients.\n\n## References\n\n- Hall, B.C. (2015) *Lie Groups, Lie Algebras, and Representations: An Elementary Introduction*. 2nd edn. Graduate Texts in Mathematics 222. Cham: Springer. [doi:10.1007/978-3-319-13467-3](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-13467-3). Chapter 2 for the matrix exponential and its failures, Chapter 3 for $\\mathrm{Ad}$ and $\\mathrm{ad}$, Chapter 5 for BCH with a full proof of convergence.\n- Stillwell, J. (2008) *Naive Lie Theory*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. [doi:10.1007/978-0-387-78214-0](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-78214-0). Chapters 5 and 7; the treatment of $\\exp$ and $\\log$ is the gentlest available.\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2013) *Introduction to Smooth Manifolds*. 2nd edn. Graduate Texts in Mathematics 218. New York: Springer. [doi:10.1007/978-1-4419-9982-5](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-9982-5). Chapter 20 for one-parameter subgroups and the exponential map on a general Lie group.\n- ★ Hall, B.C. (2013) *Quantum Theory for Mathematicians*. New York: Springer. Chapter 16, for the same material compressed, with the physics conventions ($e^{i\\theta H}$ rather than $e^{\\theta X}$) laid alongside.\n- Wüstner, M. (2003) 'A connected Lie group equals the square of the exponential image', *Journal of Lie Theory*, 13, pp. 307–309. On precisely how badly $\\exp$ can fail to be surjective, and what is true instead.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell was executed. The $\\mathrm{Ad}$/$\\mathrm{ad}$ cell is written with plain two-argument functions because the natural higher-order version — functions returning `lambda` — failed inside Maxima with a Lisp-level type error.*",
   "metadata": {}
  }
 ]
